Let $\mathbb{C}^2$ be equipped with the standard holomorphic symplectic form $\omega = dz_1 \wedge dz_2$. A holomorphic map $f:\mathbb{C}^2 \to \mathbb{C}^2$ is called an entire symplectic automorphism if it is a biholomorphism of $\mathbb{C}^2$ satisfying $f^*\omega = \omega$. A point $p \in \mathbb{C}^2$ is periodic if $f^n(p) = p$ for some integer $n \ge 1$. The forward orbit of $p$ is the set $\{f^n(p) : n \ge 0\}$; it is bounded if it has compact closure in $\mathbb{C}^2$. A point $p$ is Lyapunov-stable for $f$ if for every $\varepsilon > 0$ there exists $\delta > 0$ such that $\|q-p\| < \delta$ implies $\|f^n(q) - f^n(p)\| < \varepsilon$ for all $n \ge 0$; otherwise $p$ is Lyapunov-unstable.

Construct an entire symplectic automorphism $f$ of $\mathbb{C}^2$ such that:
\begin{itemize}
\item $f$ has no periodic point in $\mathbb{C}^2$;
\item the set of Lyapunov-unstable points of $f$ whose forward orbit is bounded is non-empty.
\end{itemize}

